export const PRIVACY = "<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title>PostQueue privacy policy · PostQueue</title>\n<style>:root { color-scheme: light dark; }\nbody { font-family: -apple-system, 'Segoe UI', system-ui, sans-serif; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 auto; padding: 1.5em 1em 3em;\n       max-width: 42em; color: #1d1d1f; background: #fff; }\nh1 { font-size: 1.7em; margin: 0.4em 0 0.6em; } h2 { font-size: 1.25em; margin: 1.6em 0 0.4em; } h3 { font-size: 1.05em; margin: 1.3em 0 0.3em; }\np, li { margin: 0.5em 0; } code { font-family: Consolas, Menlo, monospace; font-size: 0.93em; background: #f2f2f4; padding: 0.05em 0.3em; border-radius: 4px; }\na { color: #0a5bd3; } nav { font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 1.5em; } nav a { margin-right: 1em; }\n@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { body { color: #e6e6e8; background: #141416; } code { background: #26262a; } a { color: #7fb3ff; } }</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<nav><a href=\"/privacy\">Privacy policy</a><a href=\"/data-deletion\">Delete your data</a><a href=\"https://github.com/DoipSter/PostQueue\">PostQueue on GitHub</a></nav>\n<h1>PostQueue privacy policy</h1>\n<p>Last updated: 27 October 2026.</p>\n<p>PostQueue is a desktop app for Windows and macOS. It posts your photos and videos to Instagram at the dates and times you choose. This page says what the app keeps, where it keeps it, and what it sends out.</p>\n<p>The short version: everything stays on your own computer, except what Instagram needs to publish a post. PostQueue has no user accounts of its own, no analytics, no crash reports, no update checks and no ads. Nobody at PostQueue can see your queue, your captions or your photos.</p>\n<h2>What stays on your computer</h2>\n<p>PostQueue keeps everything in one folder:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Windows: <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\\PostQueue</code> (usually <code>C:\\Users\\&lt;you&gt;\\AppData\\Local\\PostQueue</code>)</li>\n<li>macOS: <code>~/Library/Application Support/PostQueue</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The folder holds:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The queue</strong> (<code>posts.json</code>): the date, time, caption and settings of each post you added.</li>\n<li><strong>Copies of your files</strong> (<code>media</code> folder): a copy of each photo and video in the queue, so the originals can be moved or deleted while a post is waiting.</li>\n<li><strong>Settings</strong> (<code>settings.json</code>): your preferences, the name and ID of the Instagram account you signed in with, the Facebook Page it is linked to, and the sign-in token described below.</li>\n<li><strong>A log</strong> (<code>postqueue.log</code>): file names, times, what was posted, and error messages. It never contains the token.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>The sign-in token</h3>\n<p>When you sign in, Meta gives PostQueue a token: a long code that lets the app publish to your account. It is valid for about 60 days. PostQueue stores it on your computer only, protected so that other programs and other user accounts cannot read it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Windows: encrypted with Windows Data Protection (DPAPI), tied to your Windows user account.</li>\n<li>macOS: stored in your login keychain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Pressing <strong>Disconnect</strong> in <strong>Settings › Instagram</strong> deletes the token. Removing PostQueue on Facebook or Instagram (see below) cancels it on Meta's side, so it stops working even if a copy remained.</p>\n<h2>What is sent, and to whom</h2>\n<h3>Meta (Facebook and Instagram)</h3>\n<p>Signing in happens in your browser, on Meta's own pages. PostQueue never sees your password.</p>\n<p>After that, PostQueue talks to Meta's Instagram Graph API on your behalf. It sends the token, the caption of each post and a link to the photo or video. It reads the name and ID of your Instagram account, the list of Facebook Pages you manage (to find the Instagram account linked to one of them), your remaining daily publishing quota, and the result of each post. Meta handles that data under its own policies: the <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/privacy/policy/\">Meta Privacy Policy</a> and the <a href=\"https://privacycenter.instagram.com/policy\">Instagram Data Policy</a>.</p>\n<h3>The PostQueue service</h3>\n<p>This applies when you used the built-in sign-in (the three-screen setup). The PostQueue service is a small program hosted on Cloudflare. It does two things a desktop app cannot do on its own:</p>\n<p>1. <strong>It finishes the sign-in.</strong> Meta's sign-in page hands the app a short-lived code. The service exchanges it for the 60-day token and returns the token to the app. The service keeps nothing: no code, no token, no record of who signed in. 2. <strong>It hosts each photo or video for a moment.</strong> Instagram's API cannot take a file upload; it needs a public link it can fetch. So, at posting time, PostQueue uploads the file to the service under a random, unguessable name, tells Instagram the link, and deletes the file right after the post is published. If the app could not delete it (for example because the computer was switched off mid-post), an hourly job deletes anything older than a day. So a file is on the service for a few seconds in the normal case, and for at most 24 hours in the worst case. While it is there, anyone who knows the exact link can fetch it, which is what lets Instagram fetch it.</p>\n<p>Only people signed in to PostQueue can upload to or delete from the service. The service stores no captions, no account names and no list of users. It is hosted on Cloudflare (Workers and R2 storage); Cloudflare processes the connection itself, including your IP address, under the <a href=\"https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/\">Cloudflare privacy policy</a>.</p>\n<h3>Your own hosting (advanced setup only)</h3>\n<p>If you chose the advanced setup, with your own Meta app and your own Cloudinary account, PostQueue uploads each file to your Cloudinary account instead of the PostQueue service, and removes it after posting. That is covered by your own agreement with Cloudinary. The PostQueue service is not used at all in that case.</p>\n<h3>Nothing else</h3>\n<p>That is the complete list. PostQueue does not send anything to anyone else, does not sell or share data, and does not build a profile of you.</p>\n<h2>Your choices</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Disconnect:</strong> Settings › Instagram › Disconnect. The token is deleted from your computer.</li>\n<li><strong>Stop PostQueue's access at Meta:</strong> on Facebook, Settings and privacy › Settings › Apps and websites › PostQueue › Remove. On Instagram, Settings › Website permissions › Apps and websites › PostQueue › Remove.</li>\n<li><strong>Delete everything:</strong> see <a href=\"/data-deletion\">Delete your data</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Children</h2>\n<p>PostQueue is for Instagram professional accounts. It is not meant for anyone under 13, and PostQueue does not knowingly handle data about children.</p>\n<h2>Changes to this policy</h2>\n<p>When this page changes, the date at the top changes with it. Earlier versions are in the project's history on GitHub.</p>\n<h2>Contact</h2>\n<p>Questions about privacy: open an issue at <a href=\"https://github.com/DoipSter/PostQueue/issues\">https://github.com/DoipSter/PostQueue/issues</a>.</p>\n</body>\n</html>\n";

export const DATA_DELETION = "<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title>Deleting your PostQueue data · PostQueue</title>\n<style>:root { color-scheme: light dark; }\nbody { font-family: -apple-system, 'Segoe UI', system-ui, sans-serif; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 auto; padding: 1.5em 1em 3em;\n       max-width: 42em; color: #1d1d1f; background: #fff; }\nh1 { font-size: 1.7em; margin: 0.4em 0 0.6em; } h2 { font-size: 1.25em; margin: 1.6em 0 0.4em; } h3 { font-size: 1.05em; margin: 1.3em 0 0.3em; }\np, li { margin: 0.5em 0; } code { font-family: Consolas, Menlo, monospace; font-size: 0.93em; background: #f2f2f4; padding: 0.05em 0.3em; border-radius: 4px; }\na { color: #0a5bd3; } nav { font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 1.5em; } nav a { margin-right: 1em; }\n@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { body { color: #e6e6e8; background: #141416; } code { background: #26262a; } a { color: #7fb3ff; } }</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<nav><a href=\"/privacy\">Privacy policy</a><a href=\"/data-deletion\">Delete your data</a><a href=\"https://github.com/DoipSter/PostQueue\">PostQueue on GitHub</a></nav>\n<h1>Deleting your PostQueue data</h1>\n<p>Last updated: 27 October 2026.</p>\n<p>PostQueue keeps your data on your own computer, not on a server. So there is nothing to ask us to delete: you delete it yourself, in a few minutes, and it is gone. The <a href=\"/privacy\">privacy policy</a> says what the data is.</p>\n<h2>1. Quit PostQueue</h2>\n<p>Right-click the PostQueue icon in the notification area (Windows, next to the clock) or the menu bar (Mac) and choose <strong>Quit</strong>. Closing the window alone is not enough: the app keeps running in the background.</p>\n<p>If you want to keep the app but remove your sign-in, open <strong>Settings › Instagram › Disconnect</strong> instead and skip to step 4.</p>\n<h2>2. Uninstall the app (optional)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Windows: Settings › Apps › Installed apps › PostQueue › Uninstall. (The portable <code>.exe</code> has nothing to uninstall; just delete the file.)</li>\n<li>macOS: drag PostQueue from the Applications folder to the Trash.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Uninstalling removes the program but leaves the data folder, so continue with step 3.</p>\n<h2>3. Delete the data folder</h2>\n<p>Everything PostQueue kept is in one folder: the queue, the copies of your photos and videos, your settings, the sign-in token and the log.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Windows:</strong> open File Explorer, paste <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\\PostQueue</code> into the address bar and press Enter. Go up one level and delete the <strong>PostQueue</strong> folder. The sign-in token is inside this folder; nothing is kept anywhere else.</li>\n<li><strong>macOS:</strong> in Finder, choose Go › Go to Folder, paste <code>~/Library/Application Support/PostQueue</code> and press Enter. Go up one level and delete the <strong>PostQueue</strong> folder. Then open <strong>Keychain Access</strong>, search for <code>PostQueue</code>, and delete the items it finds (the sign-in token lives there).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Empty the Recycle Bin or Trash if you want the files gone for good.</p>\n<h2>4. Remove PostQueue's access to your Instagram account</h2>\n<p>This cancels the sign-in on Meta's side, so no copy of the token could be used again.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Facebook</strong> (if you signed in with Facebook Login): Settings and privacy › Settings › Apps and websites › PostQueue › <strong>Remove</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Instagram</strong> (if you signed in with Instagram Login): Settings › Website permissions › Apps and websites › Active › PostQueue › <strong>Remove</strong>. The exact menu names change from time to time; look for \"Apps and websites\".</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What is not on your computer</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A file being posted right now.</strong> With the built-in sign-in, each photo or video sits on the PostQueue service only for the moment it takes Instagram to fetch it. It is deleted right after the post goes out, and anything left behind is deleted within 24 hours by an hourly job. Nothing else about you is kept on the service.</li>\n<li><strong>Posts that already went out.</strong> They are on Instagram, like any other post. Delete them in the Instagram app if you want them gone.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Contact</h2>\n<p>If something is unclear, open an issue at <a href=\"https://github.com/DoipSter/PostQueue/issues\">https://github.com/DoipSter/PostQueue/issues</a>.</p>\n</body>\n</html>\n";
